course: physics
lesson: 30
title: Compasses and the magnetic Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The whole Earth behaves like a giant magnet, and a compass needle is simply a little magnet free to turn, so it lines up with the Earth's pull and points north.
explain: The Earth's magnetism comes from deep inside, where hot liquid iron swirls in the outer core like a giant dynamo.
explain: The compass points to the magnetic north, which is not quite the same place as the North Pole at the top of the globe.
explain: Put a compass next to a fridge magnet or a steel table and it goes wrong, because the close magnet pulls harder than the far-off Earth.
explain: The Earth's magnetic field also guides charged bits from the Sun down near the poles, where they make the air glow as the northern lights, and many birds can feel the field and use it to find their way.
fact: A compass needle is a small magnet free to turn, and it lines up with the Earth's magnetic field.
fact: A compass points to magnetic north, which is not exactly the same place as the geographic North Pole.
fact: A nearby magnet or piece of steel pulls the needle more strongly than the Earth and makes the compass wrong.
fact: A magnetised needle on a leaf floating in water turns to point north; sailors used compasses like this nearly a thousand years ago.
fact: The Earth's magnetism is made by hot, liquid iron swirling in its outer core.
fact: The Earth's magnetic poles have swapped places many times in its history, over hundreds of thousands of years.
fact: Northern lights happen when charged bits from the Sun are guided by the Earth's magnetism down near the poles and make the high air glow; elsewhere the field shields us from them, and without it, over a very long time, they would strip away some of our air.
fact: Many birds, and also turtles and some fish, can sense the Earth's magnetic field and use it with the Sun and stars to find their way.
fact: A hiker lines up a map with a compass to choose the right path; a compass needs no batteries or phone signal.
fact: The magnetic strip on a bank card stores numbers as patterns of tiny magnetic areas, and a strong magnet can wipe it.
fact: An MRI scanner in hospital uses an extremely strong magnet and radio waves to make pictures of the inside of the body, so metal must stay outside the room.
fact: A loudspeaker uses electricity in a coil next to a magnet to push a cone in and out, making sound waves in the air.
exercise: Why does a compass needle point north? | It is a little magnet that lines up with the Earth's magnetism | The North Pole is made of solid iron | The wind always blows from the north | The needle is painted red at one end
exercise: Where exactly does a compass point? | To magnetic north, which is not quite the North Pole | Exactly to the North Pole every single time, without fail | To the North Star in the sky | To the nearest big city
exercise: Why does a compass go wrong next to a steel table? | The close steel pulls the needle harder than the Earth | Steel tables send out radio waves | The table blocks the view of the sky | Steel makes the needle much too heavy for it to turn round
exercise: How could you make a simple compass at home? | Float a magnetised needle on a leaf in a bowl of water | Spin a pencil on the kitchen table and see which way it stops | Hang a spoon from a piece of string | Put a coin on a plate of sand
exercise: What makes the Earth a magnet? | Hot liquid iron swirling in its outer core | A huge bar magnet buried at the North Pole | The ice at the poles | The Moon pulling on the oceans
exercise: What has happened to the Earth's magnetic poles in the past? | They have swapped places many times | They have never moved at all | They swap every year at New Year | They disappeared completely once a century
exercise: Why are the northern lights seen mostly near the poles? | The Earth's magnetism guides charged bits from the Sun down there | It is colder there, and cold air glows | The ice reflects moonlight into the sky | People there light huge bonfires at night
exercise: How do many birds find their way over thousands of kilometres? | They sense the Earth's magnetic field and use the Sun and stars | They follow the smell of the sea all the way | They just fly in one straight line until they happen to land somewhere | They follow roads and railways
exercise: Which other animals use the Earth's magnetism to find their way? | Turtles and some fish | Cats and dogs at home | Worms and snails | Spiders and flies
exercise: Why do hikers still carry compasses? | A compass works without batteries or a phone signal | A compass tells you the time of day as well as north | A compass helps to keep wild animals like bears far away | A compass shows where the nearest fresh water can be found
exercise: How does a hiker use a map and a compass together? | Turns the map so it matches north on the compass | Puts the compass on top to make the map easier to read | Reads the distances off the compass needle | Points the compass at the hill on the map
exercise: Why should you keep a bank card away from strong magnets? | A strong magnet can wipe its magnetic strip | The card will stick to the magnet forever after | The money will move across onto another card | The card will heat up and melt in your wallet
exercise: What does an MRI scanner use to take pictures of the inside of the body? | An extremely strong magnet and radio waves | Ordinary light shone right through the skin | Loud sounds bounced off the bones and back again | A very hot, bright lamp held close to the body
exercise: Why must you take off metal before an MRI scan? | The magnet is so strong it would pull on metal things | Metal makes the pictures come out far too bright to see | Metal gets cold in the scanner | The scanner would steal the metal
exercise: How does a loudspeaker make sound? | A coil with electricity next to a magnet pushes a cone in and out | A tiny person inside sings very loudly | The plastic case shakes because of the heat | Air is blown out through holes by a fan
test: bro my compass is broken, it's pointing at my phone case magnet lol. is it? | Not broken, the close magnet just pulls harder than the Earth | Yes, compasses can only break that way | Yes, it needs new batteries | No, your phone case is actually north
test: Ollie says a compass points straight to the North Pole. What's the more exact truth? | It points to magnetic north, which is a little way off | He is exactly right, every time | It points to the Sun at midday | It points to whichever way you happen to be walking at the time
test: In a cartoon, sailors use a compass made of a wooden stick. What's wrong? | The needle must be a magnet, and wood is not magnetic | Wood is too heavy to float on water | Nothing, any wooden stick will point north if it floats | Sticks only point south
test: What would happen to a compass on a ship made of steel if nobody corrected for it? | It could point the wrong way because of the ship's iron | It would work better than on land | It would spin round and round forever and never settle down | It would point at the Moon
test: Granny saw the northern lights from Scotland. What made them? | Charged bits from the Sun hitting the high air near the poles | Sunlight bouncing off the Arctic ice | Big city lights reflected by clouds | Lightning far away over the sea
test: If the Earth had no magnetic field, what might happen over a very long time? | The Sun's charged bits would strip away some of our air | The Earth would stop going round the Sun and drift off into space | Rain would fall upwards | Compasses would point south instead
test: Why might a racing pigeon get confused over a big lump of magnetic iron ore? | It uses the Earth's magnetic field to steer, and that can be disturbed | Pigeons are scared of rocks | The rocks block the smell of home | Pigeons only follow motorways
test: Dad says the Earth is a magnet because there's a giant magnet stone at the North Pole. True? | No, it comes from liquid iron moving deep in the core | Yes, a huge magnet rock sits there | Yes, it is under the ice at the South Pole | No, it is the Moon's pull that makes the whole Earth magnetic
test: shut up, my mum's going in the MRI with her earrings, so what? | She must take them off; the huge magnet pulls metal hard | Fine, earrings are too small to matter | Fine, the magnet is switched off for women | She needs to wear more metal so the machine can see her better
test: Why could you still find your way with a compass if your phone died in the hills? | A compass needs no batteries or signal | A compass can also charge up a dead phone | The compass talks to satellites in space | The hills recharge the compass as you walk
test: Which old discovery was first used by sailors nearly a thousand years ago? | A magnetised needle floating to point north | Satellite navigation worked out on a mobile phone | The radio weather forecast read out for ships at sea | A battery-powered torch for signalling to other ships
exam: Identify the origin of the magnetic field of the Earth. | The motion of molten iron within the outer core | A permanent bar magnet situated at the North Pole | The gravitational attraction of the Moon | The accumulation of ice at the polar regions
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a compass ought not to be employed in proximity to a steel object. | The steel exerts a stronger influence upon the needle than the Earth | The steel deprives the compass of the electrical supply upon which it depends | The steel renders the needle too heavy to rotate | The steel reverses the direction of the Earth's rotation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the direction indicated by a compass needle? | Magnetic north, which differs somewhat from the geographic North Pole | The geographic North Pole precisely and without exception | The position of the Pole Star at all hours | The direction in which the observer is facing
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the aurora. | Charged particles from the Sun are guided towards the poles and cause the upper air to glow | Moonlight is reflected from the polar ice upon the clouds | Distant lightning illuminates the night sky | The cold polar air emits light of its own accord
